1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,461
This is the Football Dashboard, a read-only web console for the
Football Intelligence backend.

2
00:00:05,811 --> 00:00:12,954
It answers the questions you ask when something is live: what is
the API serving, how fast, what is failing,

3
00:00:12,954 --> 00:00:16,393
and what did the AI assistant do behind each answer.

4
00:00:16,743 --> 00:00:24,081
Everything in this video is a real run of the console on its
bundled sample data, started with one command on a laptop.

5
00:00:24,933 --> 00:00:26,554
First, how it fits together.

6
00:00:26,904 --> 00:00:31,334
The backend writes structured logs, traces and errors to Google
Cloud,

7
00:00:31,334 --> 00:00:36,334
and the Android app's crashes are exported to BigQuery by
Firebase Crashlytics.

8
00:00:36,684 --> 00:00:44,172
The console only reads them, through one of three sources:
sample fixtures, the backend's own log file, or Google Cloud.

9
00:00:44,522 --> 00:00:51,306
Both sides agree on a versioned telemetry contract, version
1.1.0, with 16 event types.

10
00:00:51,656 --> 00:00:57,053
The console loads that contract at startup and relies only on
the events and fields it lists.

11
00:00:57,403 --> 00:01:03,301
The contract also forbids personal fields such as emails,
passwords and question text;

12
00:01:03,301 --> 00:01:05,702
users appear only as salted hashes.

13
00:01:06,052 --> 00:01:11,435
The stack is deliberately small: Python, FastAPI,
server-rendered templates,

14
00:01:11,435 --> 00:01:15,118
htmx for partial updates and ECharts for the charts.

15
00:01:15,969 --> 00:01:19,446
To run it, install with uv and start the demo module.

16
00:01:19,796 --> 00:01:26,120
The launcher binds the loopback address itself, and the app
refuses any connection that is not from this machine,

17
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:27,519
whatever the headers say.

18
00:01:27,869 --> 00:01:33,608
Every page is labelled as sample data, so a demonstration can
never be mistaken for production.

19
00:01:34,459 --> 00:01:41,435
The Overview page starts with what needs attention: here the
assistant is degraded and data freshness has failed.

20
00:01:41,785 --> 00:01:47,977
Below that are the headline numbers, requests, error rate, p95
latency and server crashes,

21
00:01:47,977 --> 00:01:50,660
each compared with the previous window.

22
00:01:51,010 --> 00:01:58,327
Every page shares a time window picker, and all clocks are shown
in India Standard Time while the data stays in UTC.

23
00:01:58,677 --> 00:02:03,532
The API release table compares releases from the same samples as
the summary,

24
00:02:03,532 --> 00:02:07,189
and keeps unknown releases visible instead of hiding them.

25
00:02:07,539 --> 00:02:15,710
Then traffic by status, latency percentiles, and the slowest
routes. The assistant chat route stands out, at 86 seconds.

26
00:02:16,559 --> 00:02:20,695
Requests lists every call with its release, route, status and
duration,

27
00:02:20,695 --> 00:02:24,772
and the small numbers elsewhere link straight into this filtered
list.

28
00:02:25,122 --> 00:02:27,341
Let's open that slow assistant request.

29
00:02:27,691 --> 00:02:33,842
The detail page joins the request's log events into one
timeline: the embedding call succeeded,

30
00:02:33,842 --> 00:02:40,576
then the chat model hit three connection errors, with two
retries, before the request failed with a 503.

31
00:02:40,926 --> 00:02:45,364
Underneath, the trace waterfall shows exactly where the 86
seconds went.

32
00:02:46,214 --> 00:02:52,145
The Errors page groups server crashes by exception, and handled
errors by their error code and route.

33
00:02:52,495 --> 00:02:58,655
On the right are Cloud Run platform events, such as running out
of memory or a container exiting,

34
00:02:58,655 --> 00:03:00,687
next to any degraded components.

35
00:03:01,537 --> 00:03:04,758
The Assistant page explains how answers were produced.

36
00:03:05,108 --> 00:03:10,224
It splits them by path, router, model, or model with tools, with
latency for each,

37
00:03:10,224 --> 00:03:14,154
and shows the abstention rate and how often model calls failed.

38
00:03:14,504 --> 00:03:20,818
Then the model calls themselves, each tool the assistant used,
the slowest answers, and every fallback it took.

39
00:03:21,168 --> 00:03:23,015
Token counts are shown here;

40
00:03:23,015 --> 00:03:29,478
costs appear as an estimate in Indian rupees only when an
operator configures a rate and its date.

41
00:03:29,478 --> 00:03:31,984
There is no live exchange-rate lookup.

42
00:03:32,836 --> 00:03:39,171
Data shows how fresh each league's match data is, every daily
refresh run with its errors and fallbacks,

43
00:03:39,171 --> 00:03:42,521
the downloads behind it, and which components reloaded.

44
00:03:43,901 --> 00:03:50,454
Access covers sign-in outcomes, lockouts and blocks, rate
limiting, and invite and consent events.

45
00:03:50,454 --> 00:03:53,864
People only ever appear as pseudonymous references.

46
00:03:54,713 --> 00:04:01,864
App crashes reads the Android app's Crashlytics export: fatal
crashes, non-fatals and app-not-responding events,

47
00:04:01,864 --> 00:04:03,780
with their own version filter.

48
00:04:04,130 --> 00:04:10,594
An issue's detail shows its recent devices and the latest stack,
with the app's own first frame highlighted.

49
00:04:11,444 --> 00:04:18,639
The Source page says which telemetry source is being read and
what it can answer. A local log file, for example,

50
00:04:18,639 --> 00:04:22,943
has no traces, and pages say so rather than showing an empty
chart.

51
00:04:23,293 --> 00:04:26,279
And the console ships with both a light and a dark theme.

52
00:04:27,656 --> 00:04:34,077
Now, security. Sign-in mode defaults to Google, and the console
refuses to start without its settings.

53
00:04:34,427 --> 00:04:40,649
It uses Google's OpenID Connect code flow with PKCE, accepts
exactly one owner account,

54
00:04:40,649 --> 00:04:44,582
and issues a signed session that expires after an hour.

55
00:04:44,932 --> 00:04:49,583
A default-deny guard covers pages, partial updates, details and
writes.

56
00:04:49,933 --> 00:04:56,445
The sample-data mode you've been watching is only allowed on
loopback, and refuses a real data source,

57
00:04:56,445 --> 00:04:58,807
the invite page or a hosted revision.

58
00:04:59,157 --> 00:05:03,531
The only page that writes anything, the invite page, is off by
default.

59
00:05:04,380 --> 00:05:10,637
Deployment is to Cloud Run as a private service, under a reader
service account with read-only roles,

60
00:05:10,637 --> 00:05:14,044
including a custom trace role with a single permission.

61
00:05:14,394 --> 00:05:19,412
The deploy script builds an image and creates an unserved,
private candidate,

62
00:05:19,412 --> 00:05:22,735
pinned by image digest and numeric secret versions.

63
00:05:23,085 --> 00:05:27,011
An operator checks that candidate privately and records what
they saw.

64
00:05:27,361 --> 00:05:35,156
Promotion needs that record, and the exact revision and digest,
before any traffic moves. Rollback names a revision too,

65
00:05:35,156 --> 00:05:36,001
never latest.

66
00:05:36,851 --> 00:05:43,251
Quality is enforced in CI on every pull request: Ruff, Black,
strict mypy, and the test suite.

67
00:05:43,601 --> 00:05:51,430
On this commit that is 1,665 tests at 98% coverage, against an
80% gate.

68
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:58,813
CI also builds the container image and runs ShellCheck over the
deploy scripts. Tests run offline,

69
00:05:58,813 --> 00:06:03,620
and a size rule keeps files under 400 lines and functions under
40.

70
00:06:03,970 --> 00:06:08,792
Decisions are written down as architecture decision records in
the guide folder.

71
00:06:09,643 --> 00:06:14,626
That's the Football Dashboard: an honest, read-only view of a
live AI system,

72
00:06:14,626 --> 00:06:17,408
built to be safe to run and easy to review.

73
00:06:17,758 --> 00:06:21,641
Clone it, run the demo, and every page you've seen is on your
machine.

